  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 2.06 — Hyperbolic Geometry\n\nFor two thousand years, Euclid's fifth postulate looked like a theorem in disguise. Through a point not on a line there passes exactly one parallel — surely that follows from the other four? Generations tried to prove it, and the ones who got furthest did so by assuming its negation and hunting for a contradiction.\n\nThe contradiction never came. What Lobachevsky, Bolyai and Gauss found instead, in the 1820s and 30s, was a **consistent geometry** in which infinitely many parallels pass through the point. The fifth postulate is independent of the others.\n\nThis notebook builds that geometry explicitly, using the machinery of 2.05. The recipe is the Cayley–Klein pattern promised at the end of 2.04:\n\n> Take $\\hat{\\mathbb C}$, nominate one circle, and keep only the Möbius maps preserving it and one of its sides.\n\nNominating $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ and the upper half-plane gives the subgroup of Möbius maps with **real** coefficients,\n\n$$PSL_2(\\mathbb R) = \\left\\{ z \\mapsto \\frac{az+b}{cz+d} \\;:\\; a,b,c,d \\in \\mathbb R,\\ ad-bc = 1 \\right\\},$$\n\nand the claim is that this acts on $\\mathbb H = \\{z : \\operatorname{Im} z > 0\\}$ exactly as an isometry group acts on a geometry — with\n\n$$ds = \\frac{|dz|}{\\operatorname{Im} z}.$$\n\nWhat comes out is strange and completely consistent: infinitely many parallels, triangles whose angles sum to less than $\\pi$, a *maximum* possible triangle area, and — the result that makes it geometry rather than curiosity — **area equal to angle deficit**. That last identity is Gauss–Bonnet in disguise, and 3.08 will prove the general version.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. The upper half-plane and its metric\n\n**The space.** $\\mathbb H = \\{z \\in \\mathbb C : \\operatorname{Im} z > 0\\}$.\n\n**The group.** $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$, acting by $z \\mapsto \\dfrac{az+b}{cz+d}$ with real $a,b,c,d$ and $ad - bc = 1$.\n\nThat this group preserves $\\mathbb H$ is a one-line computation, and it is the computation that makes everything else work:\n\n$$\\operatorname{Im}\\left(\\frac{az+b}{cz+d}\\right) = \\frac{(ad-bc)\\operatorname{Im} z}{|cz+d|^2} = \\frac{\\operatorname{Im} z}{|cz+d|^2}.$$\n\nSince $|cz+d|^2 > 0$, the sign of the imaginary part is preserved: upper half-plane to upper half-plane.\n\n**The metric.** Define the length of a path by\n\n$$\\mathrm{length}(\\gamma) = \\int_\\gamma \\frac{|dz|}{\\operatorname{Im} z}.$$\n\nThis is the Euclidean length, but *weighted*: distances near the real axis count for far more than distances high up. The boundary $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ is therefore infinitely far away — it is not part of the space, and it is called the **circle at infinity** or the **ideal boundary**.\n\n**Why this metric and not another.** Because it is the one $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ preserves, and it is essentially the only one. The displayed identity above says $\\operatorname{Im}$ transforms by the factor $|cz+d|^{-2}$; a short computation shows $|dz|$ transforms by $|cz+d|^{-2}$ as well, so the ratio is invariant. The group determines the geometry — Klein's thesis, doing real work rather than being restated.\n\nLet us verify all of this.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ndeclare([a,b,c,d,x,y,t], real)$\nassume(y > 0)$\n\nzxy : x + %i*y$                       /* z written in real coordinates */\nw   : (a*zxy + b)/(c*zxy + d)$\n\n/* Im(w) = (ad − bc) Im(z) / |cz+d|²   — the key identity */\nImw  : ratsimp( imagpart(rectform(w)) )$\nclaim: ratsimp( (a*d - b*c)*y / ((c*x + d)^2 + (c*y)^2) )$\nprint(\"Im(w) computed    = \", Imw)$\nprint(\"(ad−bc)·y/|cz+d|² = \", claim)$\nprint(\"difference        = \", ratsimp(Imw - claim))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"so with ad − bc = 1 the imaginary part stays positive:\")$\nprint(\"   Im(w) = Im(z)/|cz+d|²  > 0  whenever Im(z) > 0  ∎\")$\n\n/* |dw/dz| = |ad−bc|/|cz+d|².  Differentiate in a FREE variable, not in\n   zxy, which is already bound to x + iy. */\ndwdz : ratsimp( diff((a*zf + b)/(c*zf + d), zf) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"dw/dz = \", dwdz, \"     (= (ad−bc)/(cz+d)²)\")$\nprint(\"so |dw| = |dz|/|cz+d|²  when ad − bc = 1\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"hence   |dw|/Im(w) = (|dz|/|cz+d|²)·(|cz+d|²/Im z) = |dz|/Im z\")$\nprint(\"the hyperbolic line element is invariant — PSL(2,R) acts by isometries ∎\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Im(w) computed    =  ((a*d-b*c)*y)/(c^2*y^2+c^2*x^2+2*c*d*x+d^2)\n(ad−bc)·y/|cz+d|² =  ((a*d-b*c)*y)/(c^2*y^2+c^2*x^2+2*c*d*x+d^2)\ndifference        =  0\nso with ad − bc = 1 the imaginary part stays positive:\n   Im(w) = Im(z)/|cz+d|²  > 0  whenever Im(z) > 0  ∎\ndw/dz =  (a*d-b*c)/(c^2*zf^2+2*c*d*zf+d^2)      (= (ad−bc)/(cz+d)²)\nso |dw| = |dz|/|cz+d|²  when ad − bc = 1\nhence   |dw|/Im(w) = (|dz|/|cz+d|²)·(|cz+d|²/Im z) = |dz|/Im z\nthe hyperbolic line element is invariant — PSL(2,R) acts by isometries ∎"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. Geodesics and distance\n\n**The geodesics are vertical half-lines and semicircles meeting $\\mathbb R$ at right angles.**\n\nStart with the imaginary axis. A path from $ai$ to $bi$ ($0 < a < b$) along it has length\n\n$$\\int_a^b \\frac{dy}{y} = \\ln\\frac{b}{a},$$\n\nand *any* path between those two points has length at least this, because\n\n$$\\int \\frac{|dz|}{\\operatorname{Im} z} = \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{dx^2+dy^2}}{y} \\;\\ge\\; \\int \\frac{|dy|}{y} \\;\\ge\\; \\left|\\int_a^b \\frac{dy}{y}\\right| = \\ln\\frac ba,$$\n\nwith equality only when $dx \\equiv 0$ — the vertical path. So the imaginary axis is a geodesic and $d(ai, bi) = \\ln(b/a)$.\n\nEverything else follows by symmetry. $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ acts by isometries and maps circles-and-lines to circles-and-lines while preserving angles (2.05); the imaginary axis is a line perpendicular to $\\mathbb R$; so its images are exactly the circles and lines perpendicular to $\\mathbb R$. And the group is transitive enough to reach all of them.\n\n**Two consequences worth noting immediately.**\n\nDistance grows logarithmically as you approach the boundary: $d(i, \\varepsilon i) = \\ln(1/\\varepsilon) \\to \\infty$. The boundary is infinitely far away, so $\\mathbb H$ is complete — you cannot fall off the edge.\n\nThe general distance formula is\n\n$$\\cosh d(z,w) = 1 + \\frac{|z-w|^2}{2\\operatorname{Im}z \\operatorname{Im}w},$$\n\nwhich we verify below against the axis computation, and which is manifestly symmetric.\n\n**Distance as a cross-ratio.** The geodesic through $z$ and $w$ meets the ideal boundary at two points $p, q$. Then\n\n$$d(z,w) = \\bigl|\\ln (z, w; p, q)\\bigr| ,$$\n\nthe logarithm of a cross-ratio. This is the Cayley–Klein construction in its final form: the metric, destroyed in 2.02–2.04, is rebuilt out of the one projective invariant that survived, measured against the nominated conic.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Length along the imaginary axis --- */\n/* Bind the result so the assumption list is not echoed. */\nfacts : assume(aa > 0, bb > aa)$\nprint(\"length of the vertical path from ai to bi:\")$\nprint(\"   ∫ dy/y from a to b = \", integrate(1/yy, yy, aa, bb), \"  = ln(b/a)\")$\n\n/* --- The general distance formula --- */\nhdist(z1, z2) := block([num],\n  num : ratsimp( abs(z1 - z2)^2 / (2*imagpart(z1)*imagpart(z2)) ),\n  acosh( ratsimp(1 + num) ) )$\n\n/* On the imaginary axis it must reduce to ln(b/a) */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"d(i, 4i) via the formula = \", ratsimp(hdist(%i, 4*%i)),\n      \"  = \", float(hdist(%i, 4*%i)))$\nprint(\"ln(4)                    = \", float(log(4)))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"d(i, 100i)  = \", float(hdist(%i, 100*%i)), \"     ln(100) = \", float(log(100)))$\nprint(\"d(i, i/100) = \", float(hdist(%i, %i/100)), \"     the boundary is infinitely far\")$\n\n/* Isometry check: apply a PSL(2,R) element and the distance is unchanged */\ng(z) := ratsimp( (3*z + 2)/(4*z + 3) )$      /* det = 9 − 8 = 1 */\np1 : %i$  p2 : 2 + 3*%i$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"d(p1, p2)            = \", float(hdist(p1, p2)))$\nprint(\"d(g(p1), g(p2))      = \", float(hdist(g(p1), g(p2))))$\nprint(\"   images: \", rectform(g(p1)), \" and \", rectform(g(p2)))$\n\n/* --- Distance as a logarithm of a cross-ratio --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"geodesic = imaginary axis, ideal endpoints 0 and ∞\")$\nprint(\"   (ai, bi; 0, ∞) degenerates to a/b, so |ln| = ln(b/a)\")$\nprint(\"   for a = 1, b = 4:  |ln(1/4)| = \", float(abs(log(1/4))),\n      \"     d(i, 4i) = \", float(hdist(%i, 4*%i)))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "length of the vertical path from ai to bi:\n   ∫ dy/y from a to b =  log(bb)-log(aa)   = ln(b/a)\nd(i, 4i) via the formula =  acosh(17/8)   =  1.3862943611198906\nln(4)                    =  1.3862943611198906\nd(i, 100i)  =  4.605170185988092      ln(100) =  4.605170185988092\nd(i, i/100) =  4.605170185988092      the boundary is infinitely far\nd(p1, p2)            =  1.4909963089948084\nd(g(p1), g(p2))      =  1.4909963089948084\n   images:  %i/25+18/25  and  (3*%i)/265+196/265\ngeodesic = imaginary axis, ideal endpoints 0 and ∞\n   (ai, bi; 0, ∞) degenerates to a/b, so |ln| = ln(b/a)\n   for a = 1, b = 4:  |ln(1/4)| =  1.3862943611198906      d(i, 4i) =\n                                  1.3862943611198906"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Parallels, and the failure of the fifth postulate\n\nTake the geodesic $L$ = the imaginary axis, and the point $P = 2i + 1$ off it. How many geodesics through $P$ miss $L$ entirely?\n\nIn Euclidean geometry: exactly one. Here: **infinitely many**. The geodesics through $P$ are semicircles centred on $\\mathbb R$ (and one vertical line), and a whole open interval of them fails to meet the imaginary axis. Two of them are special — the ones meeting $L$ *at the ideal boundary*, at $0$ and at $\\infty$. These are called the **asymptotic parallels**, and they bound the family. Everything strictly between them is a \"hyper-parallel\", diverging from $L$ in both directions.\n\nSo the fifth postulate fails, and fails by a lot. There is not one parallel but a continuum of them, and the honest conclusion — resisted for two millennia — is that the postulate was never a consequence of the others.\n\n## 4. Area equals angle deficit\n\nThe area element that goes with $ds = |dz|/\\operatorname{Im} z$ is\n\n$$dA = \\frac{dx\\,dy}{y^2}.$$\n\nNow the central theorem of the subject:\n\n> **For a hyperbolic triangle with angles $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$,**\n> $$\\text{Area} = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma).$$\n\nThree consequences, each strange from a Euclidean standpoint:\n\n- **Angles always sum to less than $\\pi$**, since area is positive. The deficit *is* the area.\n- **There is a largest possible triangle.** Area is at most $\\pi$, attained when all three angles are zero — an **ideal triangle**, with all vertices on the boundary. No triangle, however \"big\", exceeds area $\\pi$.\n- **Similar triangles are congruent.** Angles determine area, so you cannot scale a triangle up while keeping its shape. Hyperbolic geometry has an absolute unit of length, built in.\n\nThe proof below computes the area directly for a triangle with one ideal vertex, and checks the identity against the angles computed from the geometry.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- The ideal triangle with vertices −1, 1, ∞ --- */\n/* Bounded by the verticals x = ±1 and the unit semicircle. */\ninner : integrate( 1/yv^2, yv, sqrt(1 - xv^2), inf )$\nprint(\"inner integral ∫ dy/y² from √(1−x²) to ∞ = \", inner)$\narea_ideal : integrate( inner, xv, -1, 1 )$\nprint(\"area of the ideal triangle = \", area_ideal)$\nprint(\"π − (0 + 0 + 0) = \", %pi, \"   ✓ all three angles are zero\")$\n\n/* --- A triangle with ONE ideal vertex, at ∞ --- */\n/* Verticals at x = a0 and x = b0, closed below by the unit semicircle. */\ntri_area(a0, b0) := ratsimp( integrate( integrate(1/yv^2, yv, sqrt(1-xv^2), inf),\n                                        xv, a0, b0 ) )$\n\n/* The two finite vertices sit at (a0, √(1−a0²)) and (b0, √(1−b0²)).\n   Interior angle there = angle between the upward vertical and the circle. */\nang_at_b(b0) := acos(b0)$        /* derived below */\nang_at_a(a0) := acos(-a0)$\n\nfor pr in [[-1/2, 1/2], [-4/5, 3/5], [0, 4/5]] do block([a0, b0, A, al, be],\n  a0 : pr[1], b0 : pr[2],\n  A  : tri_area(a0, b0),\n  al : ang_at_b(b0), be : ang_at_a(a0),\n  print(\"\"),\n  print(\"verticals at x = \", a0, \" and \", b0, \":\"),\n  print(\"   area              = \", float(A)),\n  print(\"   angles            = \", float(al), \", \", float(be), \", 0 (at ∞)\"),\n  print(\"   π − (α+β+γ)       = \", float(%pi - al - be)),\n  print(\"   area − deficit    = \", float(A - (%pi - al - be))) )$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "inner integral ∫ dy/y² from √(1−x²) to ∞ =  -(sqrt(1-xv^2)/(xv^2-1))\narea of the ideal triangle =  %pi\nπ − (0 + 0 + 0) =  %pi    ✓ all three angles are zero\nverticals at x =  -(1/2)  and  1/2 :\n   area              =  1.0471975511965976\n   angles            =  1.0471975511965976 ,  1.0471975511965976 , 0 (at ∞)\n   π − (α+β+γ)       =  1.0471975511965976\n   area − deficit    =  0.0\nverticals at x =  -(4/5)  and  3/5 :\n   area              =  1.5707963267948966\n   angles            =  0.9272952180016123 ,  0.6435011087932843 , 0 (at ∞)\n   π − (α+β+γ)       =  1.5707963267948966\n   area − deficit    =  0.0\nverticals at x =  0  and  4/5 :\n   area              =  0.9272952180016123\n   angles            =  0.6435011087932843 ,  1.5707963267948966 , 0 (at ∞)\n   π − (α+β+γ)       =  0.9272952180016123\n   area − deficit    =  0.0"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Where acos(b) came from.\n   The metric ds = |dz|/Im z is a POSITIVE SCALAR multiple of the Euclidean\n   one at each point, so hyperbolic angles equal Euclidean angles — the\n   model is conformal.  We may therefore measure with ordinary vectors. */\n\n/* At the vertex (b, √(1−b²)) the two sides are:\n     - the vertical geodesic, heading up to ∞ :  direction (0, 1)\n     - the unit semicircle, heading toward the other vertex (decreasing x):\n       d/dθ (cos θ, sin θ) = (−sin θ, cos θ) = (−√(1−b²), b)            */\nbsym : bq$\ndir_up   : [0, 1]$\ndir_circ : [-sqrt(1 - bsym^2), bsym]$\ncosang : ratsimp( (dir_up . dir_circ) / (sqrt(dir_up . dir_up)*sqrt(dir_circ . dir_circ)) )$\nprint(\"cos(angle at the right-hand vertex) = \", cosang)$\nprint(\"   so the angle is acos(b) — as used above\")$\n\n/* Same at the left-hand vertex, where the circle heads the other way */\nasym : aq$\ndir_circ2 : [sqrt(1 - asym^2), -asym]$\ncosang2 : ratsimp( (dir_up . dir_circ2) /\n                   (sqrt(dir_up . dir_up)*sqrt(dir_circ2 . dir_circ2)) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"cos(angle at the left-hand vertex)  = \", cosang2)$\nprint(\"   so the angle is acos(−a)\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"hence α + β = acos(b) + acos(−a) = π − (arcsin b − arcsin a) = π − Area,\")$\nprint(\"which is the theorem for a triangle with one ideal vertex.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "cos(angle at the right-hand vertex) =  bq\n   so the angle is acos(b) — as used above\ncos(angle at the left-hand vertex)  =  -aq\n   so the angle is acos(−a)\nhence α + β = acos(b) + acos(−a) = π − (arcsin b − arcsin a) = π − Area,\nwhich is the theorem for a triangle with one ideal vertex."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 5. The Poincaré disc\n\nThe half-plane is convenient for computation but lopsided to look at — one ideal point ($\\infty$) is drawn differently from all the others. The **Cayley transform**\n\n$$w = \\frac{z - i}{z + i}$$\n\nis a Möbius map carrying $\\mathbb H$ onto the unit disc $\\mathbb D$, and $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ onto the unit circle. In the disc the ideal boundary is drawn as an actual circle, and every ideal point is treated alike.\n\nBeing a Möbius map it carries geodesics to geodesics and preserves angles, so in the disc:\n\n- **geodesics** are circular arcs meeting the boundary circle at right angles, plus the diameters;\n- **the metric** becomes $\\displaystyle ds = \\frac{2|dw|}{1 - |w|^2}$;\n- the boundary circle is again infinitely far away.\n\nThis is the model Escher used for the *Circle Limit* prints. The fish do not shrink; the metric does.\n\nBoth models are conformal — angles are drawn correctly, distances are not — which is a genuine constraint rather than a design choice: no map of the hyperbolic plane into the Euclidean plane can preserve both, for exactly the reason no flat map of the Earth can.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The Cayley transform H → D */\ncayley(zz) := ratsimp( (zz - %i)/(zz + %i) )$\n\nprint(\"the Cayley transform sends:\")$\nfor zz in [%i, 2*%i, %i/2, 1+%i, 0, inf] do\n  print(\"   \", zz, \" ↦ \",\n        if zz = inf then 1 else rectform(cayley(zz)),\n        \"     |w| = \", if zz = inf then 1 else float(abs(cayley(zz))))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"i (the 'centre') ↦ 0, and the boundary R ∪ {∞} ↦ the unit circle.\")$\n\n/* Points of H land strictly inside the disc: |w|² < 1 */\nzg  : xg + %i*yg$\nwg  : (zg - %i)/(zg + %i)$\nm2  : ratsimp( expand( realpart(rectform(wg))^2 + imagpart(rectform(wg))^2 ) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"|w|² for z = x + iy: \", m2)$\nprint(\"1 − |w|²           = \", ratsimp(1 - m2))$\nprint(\"   positive exactly when y > 0 — so H maps into the open disc ∎\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "the Cayley transform sends:\n    %i  ↦  0      |w| =  0.0\n    2*%i  ↦  1/3      |w| =  0.3333333333333333\n    %i/2  ↦  -(1/3)      |w| =  0.3333333333333333\n    %i+1  ↦  1/5-(2*%i)/5      |w| =  0.4472135954999579\n    0  ↦  -1      |w| =  1.0\n    inf  ↦  1      |w| =  1\ni (the 'centre') ↦ 0, and the boundary R ∪ {∞} ↦ the unit circle.\n|w|² for z = x + iy:  (yg^2-2*yg+xg^2+1)/(yg^2+2*yg+xg^2+1)\n1 − |w|²           =  (4*yg)/(yg^2+2*yg+xg^2+1)\n   positive exactly when y > 0 — so H maps into the open disc ∎"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Geodesics through P = 1 + 2i, against the geodesic L = the imaginary axis.\n   A semicircle geodesic centred at (c,0) through P has radius r = √((1−c)²+4);\n   its ideal endpoints are c ± r.  It misses L exactly when both are positive. */\nrad(c0) := sqrt((1 - c0)^2 + 4)$\narcpts(c0) := block([r, ts],\n  r  : float(rad(c0)),\n  ts : makelist(%pi*k/240, k, 1, 239),\n  [discrete, map(lambda([th], float(c0) + r*cos(th)), ts),\n             map(lambda([th], r*sin(th)), ts)] )$\n\nprint(\"   centre   radius   ideal endpoints        meets L?\")$\nfor c0 in [0, 1, 5/2, 3, 4] do block([r],\n  r : float(rad(c0)),\n  print(\"   \", float(c0), \"     \", r, \"    \",\n        [float(c0) - r, float(c0) + r], \"   \",\n        if float(c0) - r < 0 then \"crosses\"\n        elseif abs(float(c0) - r) < 1e-12 then \"ASYMPTOTIC (meets at 0)\"\n        else \"misses — a parallel\") )$\n\nvert(x0, ymax) := [discrete, [float(x0), float(x0)], [0.001, ymax]]$\n\nplot2d([ vert(0, 7),                       /* L, the imaginary axis        */\n         arcpts(0), arcpts(1),             /* two geodesics that cross L   */\n         arcpts(5/2),                      /* asymptotic parallel (via 0)  */\n         vert(1, 7),                       /* asymptotic parallel (via ∞)  */\n         arcpts(3), arcpts(4),             /* hyper-parallels              */\n         [discrete, [1.0], [2.0]] ],       /* the point P                  */\n       [x, -3, 8], [y, 0, 7],\n       [style, [lines,3],\n               [lines,1],[lines,1],\n               [lines,2],[lines,2],\n               [lines,1],[lines,1],\n               [points,4]],\n       [legend, false], [same_xy, true],\n       [title, \"through P: infinitely many geodesics missing the imaginary axis\"])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "   centre   radius   ideal endpoints        meets L?\n    0.0       2.23606797749979      [-2.23606797749979,2.23606797749979]\n   crosses\n    1.0       2.0      [-1.0,3.0]     crosses\n    2.5       2.5      [0.0,5.0]     ASYMPTOTIC (meets at 0)\n    3.0       2.8284271247461907      [0.17157287525380926,5.828427124746191]\n       misses — a parallel\n    4.0       3.605551275463989      [0.3944487245360109,7.60555127546399]\n    misses — a parallel"
          ]
