    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n| | Half-plane $\\mathbb H$ | Disc $\\mathbb D$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| space | $\\operatorname{Im} z > 0$ | $\\lvert w\\rvert < 1$ |\n| group | $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ | conjugate of it by the Cayley map |\n| metric | $ds = \\dfrac{\\lvert dz\\rvert}{\\operatorname{Im} z}$ | $ds = \\dfrac{2\\lvert dw\\rvert}{1-\\lvert w\\rvert^2}$ |\n| geodesics | vertical lines, semicircles $\\perp \\mathbb R$ | diameters, arcs $\\perp$ boundary |\n| ideal boundary | $\\mathbb R \\cup \\{\\infty\\}$ | the unit circle |\n\nEverything was derived rather than posited. The single identity\n\n$$\\operatorname{Im}\\left(\\frac{az+b}{cz+d}\\right) = \\frac{\\operatorname{Im} z}{|cz+d|^2}$$\n\ndoes three jobs at once: it shows $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ preserves $\\mathbb H$, it forces the metric $|dz|/\\operatorname{Im}z$ to be the invariant one, and it makes the group act by isometries. Klein's thesis is not decoration here — **the group picked the metric**.\n\nThe results, all checked numerically:\n\n- $d(i, 4i) = \\ln 4$, and the boundary is infinitely far ($d \\to \\infty$ as $\\operatorname{Im} \\to 0$).\n- Distance is a logarithm of a cross-ratio against the ideal boundary — the **Cayley–Klein** construction, rebuilding a metric from purely projective data.\n- Through a point off a line pass **infinitely many** non-intersecting geodesics: two asymptotic parallels bounding a continuum of hyper-parallels.\n- **Area $= \\pi - (\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)$**, verified to machine zero for three triangles, with the ideal triangle having area exactly $\\pi$.\n\n## Why the area identity is the important one\n\nAngle deficit equals area. Read it as a statement about **curvature** and it becomes the local version of a theorem we will meet twice more:\n\n$$\\underbrace{\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma - \\pi}_{\\text{excess}} \\;=\\; \\int\\!\\!\\int_T K\\,dA, \\qquad K = -1 \\text{ here.}$$\n\n- In **spherical** geometry (2.07) the same formula holds with $K = +1$: angle *excess* equals area, which is Girard's theorem.\n- In **Euclidean** geometry $K = 0$ and the excess is zero — the angle sum is exactly $\\pi$.\n- On a **general surface** (3.08) this becomes the Gauss–Bonnet theorem, with $K$ varying from point to point.\n\nSo the three geometries of Stage 2 are the three constant-curvature cases of one theorem, and the trichotomy $\\sum 1/n_i \\gtrless 1$ from 1.04 was the same trichotomy in disguise. Hyperbolic geometry is not an exotic alternative to Euclidean geometry; it is the $K < 0$ member of a family.\n\nThat is also where the Erlangen picture reaches its limit. These three are the only *homogeneous* surfaces — the only ones with enough symmetry for a transformation group to define the geometry. Stage 3 abandons homogeneity, lets $K$ vary, and has to rebuild geometry locally. Riemann's programme begins exactly where Klein's runs out.\n\n## Next\n\n**2.07 — Spherical and Elliptic Geometry** does the positive-curvature case: great circles as geodesics, no parallels at all, and area $= \\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma-\\pi$. Placing it beside this notebook completes the trichotomy and closes Stage 2.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Hartshorne, R. (2000) *Geometry: Euclid and Beyond*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-0-387-22676-7. Chapter 7 is the fullest treatment here: §33 history of the parallel postulate, §36 non-Euclidean area, §37 circular inversion, §39 the Poincaré model, §40 hyperbolic geometry, §42 hyperbolic trigonometry.\n- ★ Lee, N.-H. (2020) *Geometry: from Isometries to Special Relativity*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42101-4. Chapter 4 is the hyperbolic plane, developed from isometries exactly as here, and Chapter 5 carries it into special relativity.\n- ★ Stillwell, J. (2005) *The Four Pillars of Geometry*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/0-387-29052-4. Chapter 8 on the hyperbolic plane, reached via the projective route of Chapters 5–6.\n- Anderson, J.W. (2005) *Hyperbolic Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. A short undergraduate book devoted entirely to the upper half-plane model — the closest single match to this notebook.\n- Beardon, A.F. (1983) *The Geometry of Discrete Groups*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 91. New York: Springer. The reference for $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$, its classification into elliptic, parabolic and hyperbolic elements, and Fuchsian groups.\n\nThe independence of the parallel postulate was established in the 1820s–30s by Lobachevsky and Bolyai, with Gauss having reached similar conclusions privately and not published them.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
